\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Polynomial functions do not depend on a basis}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For a finite-dimensional real vector space #{E}, the actual polynomial
function algebra used in \ref{fdip-002A} is independent of coordinates.
It is the real unital subalgebra of functions #{E\to\mathbb R} generated
by linear functionals. In a chosen basis, every linear functional is a
finite linear combination of coordinate evaluations. Conversely every
coordinate evaluation is linear. Thus these generators give exactly the
coordinate polynomial algebra of that note. Substituting linear
combinations in a finite polynomial gives a finite polynomial, so changing
basis preserves the algebra.}
\p{Its operations and identity involution are pointwise, and its functions
are continuous, hence Borel for the finite-dimensional topology.
For #{E=0}, the algebra consists of constants. These are actual functions;
[formal polynomial expressions](ag-0004) and their
[polynomial rings](ag-0007) are related algebraic vocabulary, not a
second choice of observable functions here.}
\p{This coordinate-independence argument is a direct deduction from the
generators. The algebra and star conventions are those already used in
\ref{fdip-002A}. For these conventions, see
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks} and
\citet{Definition 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
Neither citation asserts Gaussian integrability.}
